    "#### Analysis of Violin Plots\n",
    "\n",
    "Visually, the control group violin (outside the 2-mile radius) bows out more widely around the first quartile and median, indicating a greater concentration of data points in that range. The treatment group violin, by contrast, is more evenly distributed across its range, reflecting greater income variation among zip codes near stadiums. While the shapes are broadly comparable, the first quartile and median appear slightly higher in the control group, whereas the third quartile appears marginally higher in the treatment group.\n",
    "\n",
    "This pattern is confirmed by the summary statistics. The treatment group has a median household income of \$77,727 and a mean of \$86,322, compared to \$80,898 and \$87,733 for the control group, differences of approximately \$3,171 and \$1,411, respectively. The treatment group also exhibits a higher standard deviation (\$40,990 vs. \$37,488), reflecting greater income heterogeneity within zip codes near stadiums. While some stadium-adjacent areas are relatively wealthier, others are considerably less so.\n",
    "\n",
    "Taken together, these findings show that areas within two miles of a stadium are slightly less wealthy than areas further away. The differences are moderate, roughly 4% lower median income and 1.6% lower mean income. Statistical significance testing, such as a t-test, is needed to confirm whether these differences are meaningful. Additionally, this is an observational comparison and does not establish whether stadium proximity causes lower incomes, or whether stadiums are more likely to be situated in areas that were already slightly less wealthy.\n"
